GRRLIB patch: keep the system font loaded across GRRLIB_Exit()/GRRLIB_Init().

Applied after grrlib_reentrant_init_patch.diff (see Dockerfile.dev). WiiFin
calls GRRLIB_Exit()/GRRLIB_Init() around every video playback session. Stock
GRRLIB_Exit() frees the system font and the next GRRLIB_Init() reloads it via
libogc's SYS_InitFont(), which reads the packed (Yay0) font from the boot ROM
with EXI DMA.

EXI DMA can only address MEM1 -- its address register is too narrow for MEM2.
After the first MPlayer session WiiFin's heap has grown into MEM2, so the
re-allocated font buffer can land there; the ROM data is then written to the
truncated address in MEM1 instead, which is where WiiFin's code lives. That
silently overwrote ~12-16 KB of code at a different address each time --
Program exceptions / random crashes on real hardware, "IntCPU: Unknown
instruction" under Dolphin with MMU emulation. Found with per-page code
checksums (source/core/CodeIntegrity.cpp) around the post-playback
GRRLIB_Init(); the overwritten page began with "Yay0".

The first load happens at app startup while the heap is still entirely in
MEM1, so keeping that font for the app's lifetime avoids the DMA entirely
from then on (GRRLIB_InitSystemFont() already returns early while loaded).

--- a/GRRLIB/GRRLIB/GRRLIB_core.c
+++ b/GRRLIB/GRRLIB/GRRLIB_core.c
@@ -259,8 +259,13 @@
         gp_fifo = NULL;
     }
 
-    // Done with the system font
-    GRRLIB_ExitSystemFont();
+    // Keep the system font loaded across GRRLIB_Exit()/GRRLIB_Init() cycles
+    // (WiiFin patch, see grrlib_keep_system_font_patch.diff): reloading it
+    // DMAs the packed font from ROM over EXI, which can only address MEM1.
+    // Once an app's heap has grown into MEM2, the re-allocated font buffer
+    // lands there and the ROM data is written to the truncated (MEM1)
+    // address instead -- over the app's own code. GRRLIB_InitSystemFont()
+    // already returns early while the font is still loaded.
 
     // Done with TTF
     GRRLIB_ExitTTF();
